From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 04 Sep 2026 09:22:29 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] audio/alsa: report the generic buffer as free, as 7.0 did

DomA -> HDMI audio does not play on RPi4 under qemu 10.0.2. The guest side is
fine: AudioFlinger writes (Frames written: 9,989,760, no underruns) and the
virtio-snd driver sends, but the host ALSA device never advances -- appl_ptr
reaches exactly one period (1920 frames) and then stops, and the guest cycles
PREPARE -> START -> STOP -> RELEASE 23 times a second.

This is not a regression of the qemu upgrade in the sense of "it worked before
on this board" -- audio on it was never verified working ("Frames written: 0"
was recorded under 7.0 on 2026-09-03). But the mechanism is a 7.0 -> 10.0.2
behaviour change, and the
device model is not at fault: hw/audio/virtio-snd.c is function-for-function
identical to the 8.2.0 implementation that plays on RPi5.

What changed is one line in the ALSA backend's op table:

    7.0     .buffer_get_free = audio_generic_buffer_get_free
    10.0.2  .buffer_get_free = alsa_buffer_get_free

audio_pcm_hw_get_free() feeds that into hw_free, and audio_run_out() turns
hw_free into the `available` it hands the device callback:

    if (hw_free > sw->total_hw_samples_mixed) { free = ...; } else { free = 0; }
    if (free > sw->resample_buf.pos) {
            free = MIN(free, sw->resample_buf.size) - sw->resample_buf.pos;
            sw->callback.fn(sw->callback.opaque, free * sw->info.bytes_per_frame);
    }

With audio_generic_buffer_get_free the answer comes from qemu's own buffer and
is essentially always positive, so virtio_snd_pcm_out_cb() runs on every timer
tick. With alsa_buffer_get_free the answer comes from snd_pcm_avail_update() on
the host device; when the host has no room, free becomes 0 and the callback is
not called at all. virtio-snd returns a TX buffer to the guest only once
AUD_write() has consumed it (return_tx_buffer() in the drain loop), so no
callback means no buffers returned, the guest's ring fills, and the guest's
ALSA gives up and closes the stream -- which is the 23 Hz open/close cycle
observed, and why the host PCM never gets past PREPARED.

Restore the 7.0 behaviour. The upstream change buys lower latency by pacing on
the real device, but that pacing assumes a frontend that can be handed a
partial `available` and make progress; virtio-snd's all-or-nothing buffer
return does not satisfy it. That leaves alsa_buffer_get_free() with no user,
so it goes too rather than stay behind as an unused static.

Verified: the audiodev timing hints tuned for 7.0's core
(out.period-length=40000 / out.buffer-length=160000 / out.try-poll=off) are NOT
the cause -- removing all three and letting 10.0.2 pick its defaults leaves the
symptom unchanged (measured on hardware 2026-09-04).

Later finding (2026-09-13): every observation above was made before DomD kernel
patch 0016, while vc4-hdmi audio programmed the DMA with the MAI FIFO's
untranslated CPU address, so the DMA never reached the FIFO. They therefore do
not show that 10.0.2's report breaks playback on its own once the DMA flows,
and the two reports have not been compared since 0016. The patch stays because
doma.cfg's audiodev values were chosen with it: its 60 ms buffer-length rests
on the backend answering from its own buffer, and was measured with qemu
10.0.2 and a virtio-media decode running. It also makes the qemu 10 lane
answer as the 7.0.0 device model of the gl lane does, so one set of audiodev
values serves both.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [platform-specific interaction with virtio-snd]
---
 audio/alsaaudio.c | 38 +-------------------------------------
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 37 deletions(-)

diff --git a/audio/alsaaudio.c b/audio/alsaaudio.c
index cacae1ea59c..34c9469cd7c 100644
--- a/audio/alsaaudio.c
+++ b/audio/alsaaudio.c
@@ -598,42 +598,6 @@ static int alsa_open(bool in, struct alsa_params_req *req,
     return -1;
 }
 
-static size_t alsa_buffer_get_free(HWVoiceOut *hw)
-{
-    ALSAVoiceOut *alsa = (ALSAVoiceOut *)hw;
-    snd_pcm_sframes_t avail;
-    size_t alsa_free, generic_free, generic_in_use;
-
-    avail = snd_pcm_avail_update(alsa->handle);
-    if (avail < 0) {
-        if (avail == -EPIPE) {
-            if (!alsa_recover(alsa->handle)) {
-                avail = snd_pcm_avail_update(alsa->handle);
-            }
-        }
-        if (avail < 0) {
-            alsa_logerr(avail,
-                        "Could not obtain number of available frames\n");
-            avail = 0;
-        }
-    }
-
-    alsa_free = avail * hw->info.bytes_per_frame;
-    generic_free = audio_generic_buffer_get_free(hw);
-    generic_in_use = hw->samples * hw->info.bytes_per_frame - generic_free;
-    if (generic_in_use) {
-        /*
-         * This code can only be reached in the unlikely case that
-         * snd_pcm_avail_update() returned a larger number of frames
-         * than snd_pcm_writei() could write. Make sure that all
-         * remaining bytes in the generic buffer can be written.
-         */
-        alsa_free = alsa_free > generic_in_use ? alsa_free - generic_in_use : 0;
-    }
-
-    return alsa_free;
-}
-
 static size_t alsa_write(HWVoiceOut *hw, void *buf, size_t len)
 {
     ALSAVoiceOut *alsa = (ALSAVoiceOut *) hw;
@@ -943,7 +907,7 @@ static struct audio_pcm_ops alsa_pcm_ops = {
     .init_out = alsa_init_out,
     .fini_out = alsa_fini_out,
     .write    = alsa_write,
-    .buffer_get_free = alsa_buffer_get_free,
+    .buffer_get_free = audio_generic_buffer_get_free,
     .run_buffer_out = audio_generic_run_buffer_out,
     .enable_out = alsa_enable_out,
 
